From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 04:03:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J: the player waits on the isolated host for a limited
 time only

A host that stops reading what the player sends held the thread that sent.
The reader of the client waited in its acknowledgement of a drawing, every
call of the player waited for the reader, and so the player's thread was held
until the call's 60 s without anything from the host ran out. A send without a
call (BDR_CLOSE when the disc closes) had no end at all. A host that never
answered a call but kept sending other things (log lines, drawings) was never
taken as hung. The greeting was waited for without end.

- bdr_send_timed(): a send that the peer must take within a time limit. With
  a limit, sendmsg() never blocks (MSG_DONTWAIT): the wait for room is poll(),
  up to the deadline, then ETIMEDOUT. bdr_send() is bdr_send_timed() without a
  limit (the host's sends, as before).
- Every send of the client has a limit of 5 s. A message the host does not
  take in that time ends the host as one that does not answer: it is killed,
  the disc goes on as when BD-J does not start, and the log says once "BD-J
  host: it stopped reading what the player sends (nothing taken in 5 s)".
  Measured with the image's units and a stream read through the host: the
  sends to a healthy host never waited for room, idle or with the host and the
  player on two processors shared with 8 and with 32 busy loops. With a send
  buffer of 8 KiB, so that most large messages wait for the host to read them,
  the longest wait was 32 ms (one processor shared with 64 busy loops). The
  host reads in a thread that does nothing else.
- The answers to names (BD-Live) go out with the same limit, and one that
  does not ends the host through the client: bdr_net_new() takes the limit
  and a callback.
- A call, or the opening of the disc, never takes longer than five times its
  limit (300 s, 600 s), whatever the host sends meanwhile.
- The answer to the greeting is waited for at most 30 s, and connect() at
  most as long (SO_SNDTIMEO, which connect() honours on a stream socket of
  this kind when the listener takes no connection).
- A host that does not take BDR_CLOSE gets no grace time to close the disc.
